Chapter: RealTrappingOverflow

 The positive half of the `Real trapping` contract: a result that leaves the
 finite range must trap.

 It traps with `UD2`, so this program dies rather than printing, and there is
 no output to compare. That is why it carries a `.fatal` sidecar and no
 `.expected`, the same arrangement `bounded-param-trap` and
 `bounded-return-trap` use for the same reason.

 The line before the overflow prints, so a run that produces `alive` and then
 dies on `!EXC=06` is the trap firing at the right place. A run that prints
 `alive` and then a bit pattern is the guard failing to fire, which is the
 defect this test exists for: the emitter checked for NaN alone and let every
 infinity through.

Section: Operands

  real-max : Real
  real-max = bits-to-real #7FEFFFFFFFFFFFFF

Section: Ops

  add-trap : Real trapping, Real trapping -> Real trapping
  add-trap (a) (b) = a + b

  trap-bits : Real trapping -> Integer
  trap-bits (x) = real-to-bits (from-real-trapping x)

Section: Report

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "alive"
    print-line-uni ("must-not-print " & show (trap-bits (add-trap (to-real-trapping real-max) (to-real-trapping real-max))))
  end
